  "query_preview": "..Northern Arizona is essentially within the same \npattern as Wednesday, but there are some subtle differences that \nwill govern convection today. First and foremost, all the rainfall \nfrom yesterday has cooled off the boundary layer, and over a fairly \nlarge area. This will inhibit some convection, or at least add a few \nhours to the initiation times. When this happens, it is much more \ndifficult to get organized and widespread convection and we end up \nwith spottier coverage. Additionally, the more dynamical portion of \nthe trough is currently lifting northeast, which may allow for some \nweak subsidence to develop over central Arizona.\n\nNow, for favorable convective support, there are also a few items \nthat we still have going for us. For northwestern Arizona, which is \ncloser to the trough axis, we still have a fairly cool mid-level \nenvironment and some shear, aiding in the development of convective \ninstability. This is evident by the convection still ongoing over \nnorthern Mohave County. This same area also has decent upper level \nsupport, which actually persists through today and into Friday. The \nmoisture plume over Arizona is also still largely intact, but has \nshifted a a bit southeastward.\n\nSo, best guess is another active day over the northwestern zones \ntoday and Friday, along with a secondary area over our east-central \nzones, which are closer to the moisture plume. All other areas will \nsee decreases in activity, entering more of a low-grade pattern. It \nis important to note that moisture levels are still high, so even \nwith the spotty storms, localized heavy rainfall remains possible. \nAlso, with the dynamical support for the northwestern zones, severe \nthunderstorm development is possible.\n\nOver the weekend and possibly into next week, the ridge to our east \nattempts to push back westward. However, the long-wave trough will \nremain off the west coast through the period, constantly maintaining \nsouthwesterly flow and constantly trying to inject drier mid-level \nair. As the ridge pushes back westward, mid-level temperatures will \nbegin to warm, potentially stabilizing a large swath of our CWA and \nleaving us with light southwesterly winds, without much in the way \nof storm coverage.\n\nFinally, late in this forecast period, we may return to a more \ntraditional pattern and Monsoon activity looks like it will pick \nback up.\n\nIn summary, active conditions today and Friday, especially for \nnorthwestern and east-central Arizona, followed by a drying trend \nand somewhat warmer temperatures over the weekend and into early \nnext week.",
